@node Differential First Program
@section Write a program, change its input

A differential program is two easy steps: write a program, then change
its input.  We will work through both with one example, the skip-level
management report.

@subsection Step 1: write a program

You write differential programs against collections that appear static,
using operations that look like database or MapReduce idioms.  Our
example has one input: a collection @code{manages} of pairs
@code{(manager, person)}.  The program finds, for each person, their
manager's manager.  In SQL this is an equijoin, and we write exactly
that: re-order @code{manages} so the person is the key, join it with
@code{manages} on the shared middle manager, and report the result.

@example
static void
swap_pair(void *ctx, const void *rec, size_t len, dtfl_collection_emit *out)
@{
        unsigned char buf[16];                  /* (m2, m1) -> (m1, m2) */
        memcpy(buf, (const unsigned char *)rec + 8, 8);
        memcpy(buf + 8, rec, 8);
        dtfl_collection_emit_record(out, buf, sizeof buf);
@}

static void
build(dtfl_scope *scope, void *arg)
@{
        /* ... */
        dtfl_collection *manages =
            dtfl_input_session_to_collection(c->manages, scope);
        dtfl_collection *swapped =
            dtfl_collection_map(manages, pair, swap_pair, DTFL_NOCTX);
        dtfl_collection *joined =
            dtfl_collection_join_map(swapped, manages, u64, u64, u64,
                triple, skip_level, DTFL_NOCTX);
        dtfl_collection_consolidate(joined);    /* ... inspect, probe */
@}
@end example

The input is loaded as a binary tree of ten people: person @var{p}
reports to person @code{p/2}.  (Only worker 0 loads the data; the next
section shows how to spread the load across workers.)

@example
for (person = 0; person < 10; person++) @{
        unsigned char buf[16];
        dtfl_put_u64(buf, person / 2);          /* manager */
        dtfl_put_u64(buf + 8, person);          /* person */
        dtfl_input_session_insert(c->manages, buf, sizeof buf);
@}
dtfl_input_session_advance_to(c->manages, &one);
dtfl_input_session_flush(c->manages);
@end example

The complete program is in @file{doc/snippets/dd_manages.c}.  Each output
record is @code{(m1, (m2, p))}: person @var{p}, their manager @var{m1},
and that manager's manager @var{m2}.  Run at time 0 it prints the
skip-level reports of the tree:

@example
(0, (0, 0))
(0, (0, 1))
(1, (0, 2))
(1, (0, 3))
(2, (1, 4))
@dots{}
(4, (2, 9))
@end example

Note two C specifics.  @code{dtfl_collection_join_map} emits exactly the
bytes the result closure writes; the key is not prepended, so the
closure writes @code{m1 || m2 || p} itself (@pxref{Records}).  And
@code{dtfl_collection_consolidate} sums the diffs per @code{(record,
time)}, so each distinct report appears once.

@subsection Step 2: change its input

The program was written against a static collection, but the collection
can change.  Suppose each employee switches from reporting to
@code{p/2} to reporting to @code{p/3}, one person per logical time.
After loading the initial tree, advance the time and apply each change:

@example
for (person = 1; person < 10; person++) @{
        uint64_t t = person;                    /* a distinct epoch */
        unsigned char old[16], new[16];

        dtfl_input_session_advance_to(c->manages, &t);
        dtfl_put_u64(old, person / 2); dtfl_put_u64(old + 8, person);
        dtfl_put_u64(new, person / 3); dtfl_put_u64(new + 8, person);
        dtfl_input_session_remove(c->manages, old, sizeof old);
        dtfl_input_session_insert(c->manages, new, sizeof new);
@}
@end example

@code{dtfl_input_session_advance_to} moves to a new logical time;
@code{dtfl_input_session_remove} retracts the old management relation
(a diff of @code{-1}) and @code{dtfl_input_session_insert} asserts the
new one (@code{+1}).  Differential dataflow responds with the changes to
the skip-level report: for each affected person it emits the retractions
(diff @code{-1}) of reports that no longer hold and the assertions (diff
@code{+1}) of the new ones, each at the time the change took effect.  It
does not recompute the whole report; it computes only the difference.
